{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b718-8bd8-7441-8147-b7f0defade56","session_id":"01a1b715-27ed-7fbb-b7b5-ecb1f10e3c39","message":{"id":"01a1b7188bd874418147b7eed5c4518e","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent. Now the check-seams script.\n\nDesign details again:\n\n- usage: `node scripts/check-seams.mjs [--root <dir>] [--json]`\n- default root = repo root\n- scan `<root>/lib/**` .mjs/.js/.cjs\n- default output: `FAIL <rule> <file>:<line>` then summary\n- --json: `{ ok, files, violations: [{rule,file,line}] }` only\n- exit 0/1/2 (2 on arg error, stderr contains 'usage:')\n- export `scanRoot({root})` → `{files, violations}`\n- only run when invoked directly\n\nFiles count = number of scanned files.\n\nOrder of violations: sorted by file then line then rule presumably. V-007-4 expects exactly 2 lines with 1 violation. V-007-3 requires all violations of one rule in bad.mjs.\n\nImplementation detail: the \"new_date\" rule needs care with `new Date(x)`.\n\nLet me write the scanner.\n\nRanges approach:\n```js\nfunction scanSource(source) -> { code: string (comments blanked), bare: string (comments + string bodies blanked), stringRanges: [[start,end]], commentRanges: [[start,end]] }\n```\n\nSingle pass:\n```js\nconst n = source.length\nlet code = ''   // accumulate with same length\n```\nTo preserve indices, build arrays of chars. Simpler: use `const code = source.split('')` and `const bare = source.split('')`, then blank out by replacing chars with ' ' (except '\\n' and '\\r').\n\nState machine:\n```\nlet i = 0\nwhile (i < n) {\n  const ch = source[i], nx = source[i+1]\n  if (ch === '/' && nx === '/') { // line comment\n    start = i\n    while (i < n && source[i] !== '\\n') { blank(i++) }\n    commentRanges.push([start, i])\n    continue\n  }\n  if (ch === '/' && nx === '*') {\n    start = i; blank(i); blank(i+1); i += 2\n    while (i < n && !(source[i] === '*' && source[i+1] === '/')) blank(i++)\n    if (i < n) { blank(i); blank(i+1); i += 2 }\n    commentRanges.push([start, i]); continue\n  }\n  if (ch === \"'\" || ch === '\"' || ch === '`') { quote = ch; start = i; i++; \n    while (i < n) { \n      if (source[i] === '\\\\') { bare[i] = ' '; bare[i+1] = ' '; i += 2; continue }\n      if (source[i] === quote) { i++; break }\n      if (source[i] === '\\n' && quote !== '`') { i++; break }  // unterminated\n      bare[i] = (source[i] === '\\n' || source[i] === '\\r') ? source[i] : ' '\n      i++\n    }\n    stringRanges.push([start, i]); continue\n  }\n  i++\n}\n```\nblank(i) sets code[i]=' ' and bare[i]=' ' unless newline.\n\nWait: for strings we blank only bare; code keeps them. For comments blank both.\n\nNote: quote chars at start/end remain in bare — harmless.\n\nRegex literal handling: not present in lib except `/\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+(?:[-+][\\w.]+)?/` in availability.mjs and `/\"/...` patterns. Hmm! `/'\"loggedin\"\\s*:\\s*true/` contains double quotes and a backslash. My scanner in normal state sees `/` then `'`? Let's trace: the regex literal `/\"loggedin\"\\s*:\\s*true/` — characters: `/`, `\"`, `l`, ... My scanner at `/` checks nx = `\"` — not `/` or `*` → treated as normal char, i++. Then `\"` starts a string → scans until next `\"`, which is after `loggedin` → string range. Then `\\s*:\\s*true/` remains normal text. This garbles code/bare a bit but doesn't create false positives for our rules. Could it create false negatives? The closing part `\\s*:\\s*true/` isn't an import or time primitive.\n\nBut wait — `bare` would lose `\"loggedin\"` only; irrelevant.\n\nMore important: `/\\blogged in\\b/` → `/` then `\\` → normal; `b`... fine.\n\nAnother: `/\\r?\\n/` in config.mjs: `/` normal, `\\`, `r`, `?`, `\\`, `n`, `/`. Fine.\n\nRisk: a regex containing `//` like `/https?:\\/\\//` — not present.\n\nOK. But there's a subtle catastrophic case: in availability.mjs the regex literal `/'\"loggedin\"\\s*:\\s*true/` — after the string closes at the second `\"`, remaining `\\s*:\\s*true/`. No issue.\n\nHmm, but what about `text.includes(String(provider.auth_ok).toLowerCase())` — fine.\n\nOne more important case in lib/index.js: `'http://local'` is inside a string → protected.\n\nOK.\n\nNow new_date detection on `bare`:\n```js\nconst NEW_DATE = /\\bnew\\s+Date\\b/g\n```\nFor each match at index i with length L: look at bare.slice(i+L). If /^\\s*\\(/ matches: examine content up to matching `)`. Since bare has string contents blanked but parens in args remain, we can check: skip spaces, if `(` then skip spaces, if next char is `)` → violation; else not.\nIf no `(` → violation.\n\nImplement:\n```js\nfunction newDateViolation(bare, index, length) {\n  let j = index + length\n  while (j < bare.length && /\\s/.test(bare[j])) j++\n  if (bare[j] !== '(') return true\n  j++\n  while (j < bare.length && /\\s/.test(bare[j])) j++\n  return bare[j] === ')'\n}\n```\n\nset_timeout rule:\n```js\nconst BARE_TIMER = /(^|[^\\w$.])(setTimeout|setInterval|setImmediate)\\b/g\n```\nand global-prefixed:\n```js\nconst GLOBAL_TIMER = /(^|[^\\w$.])(globalThis|global|window)\\s*\\.\\s*(setTimeout|setInterval|setImmediate)\\b/g\n```\nHmm, `globalThis.setTimeout` — BARE_TIMER would match at index of `setTimeout` preceded by '.', which is excluded by `[^\\w$.]`. Good. So BARE_TIMER alone covers globalThis/window forms? For `globalThis.setTimeout`, the char before setTimeout is '.', so BARE doesn't match → we need GLOBAL_TIMER.\n\nLet me just emit rule based on the identifier name.\n\nFor BARE: the leading group consumes one char; match index = m.index + m[1].length.\n\nNow `obj.setTimeout(` → preceded by '.', excluded. `foo?.setTimeout` → preceded by '.', excluded. Good.\n\nwall_clock: /performance\\s*\\.\\s*now\\b|\\bprocess\\s*\\.\\s*hrtime\\b|\\bhrtime\\s*\\.\\s*bigint\\b/\n\nprocess_exit: /\\bprocess\\s*\\.\\s*exit\\b/\n\nfs/child_process/timers import: parse specifiers from `code` (comments blanked, strings intact), filter matches inside string ranges.\n\nWait — `code` has comments blanked so a commented-out import won't match. But string contents intact, so I filter by stringRanges. Since comments are blanked, no need to filter comments.\n\nSpecifier regexes over `code`:\n```js\nconst FROM_RE = /\\bfrom\\s*(['\"])([^'\"\\n]+)\\1/g\nconst DYN_RE = /\\bimport\\s*\\(\\s*(['\"])([^'\"\\n]+)\\1/g\nconst REQUIRE_RE = /\\brequire\\s*\\(\\s*(['\"])([^'\"\\n]+)\\1/g\nconst BARE_IMPORT_RE = /(^|[\\s;])import\\s*(['\"])([^'\"\\n]+)\\2/g\n```\nFilter: index of the quote must not be inside a string range. Use the match index + offset of quote.\n\nSimpler: for each match, compute quoteIndex = m.index + m[0].indexOf(m[1])? Ambiguous. Instead test whether m.index is inside a string range; if `from 'x'` appears inside a string literal, m.index would be inside a string range. Good enough. In `code`, string contents are intact so `from 'node:fs'` inside a string would have its match index inside a string range → filtered out. \n\nBut careful: the match for a real import `import fs from 'node:fs'` — m.index is at `from`, not in a string range. Good.\n\nEdge: `const x = \"a\" + from + ...` unlikely.\n\nThen map specifier → rule and dedupe (a file may import fs twice → two violations? Design says each violation one line; dedupe by (rule,file,line) would keep both if different lines. Fine.)\n\nNow allowed lists:\n```js\nconst ALLOW = {\n  'seams.child_process': ['lib/exec.mjs'],\n  'seams.fs': ['lib/context.mjs', 'lib/store.mjs', 'lib/fsx.mjs'],\n  'seams.date_now': ['lib/context.mjs'],\n  'seams.new_date': ['lib/context.mjs'],\n  'seams.set_timeout': ['lib/context.mjs'],\n  'seams.set_interval': ['lib/context.mjs'],\n  'seams.set_immediate': ['lib/context.mjs'],\n  'seams.node_timers': ['lib/context.mjs'],\n  'seams.wall_clock': ['lib/context.mjs'],\n  'seams.process_exit': [],\n}\n```\n\nSort violations by file, line, rule.\n\nFiles count: number of scanned files (all .mjs/.js/.cjs under lib).\n\nRecursive walk with sorted entries for determinism.\n\nCLI:\n```js\nfunction main() {\n  const args = process.argv.slice(2)\n  let root = REPO_ROOT, json = false\n  for (...) { '--root' → value; '--json' → json; else usage error }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nOn arg error: `process.stderr.write(`unknown argument: ${flag}\\n${USAGE}\\n`)`, exitCode 2, return.\n\nOutput default:\n```\nfor (v of violations) out(`FAIL ${v.rule} ${v.file}:${v.line}\\n`)\nout(violations.length ? `check-seams: FAILED (${n} violations)\\n` : `check-seams: ok (files=${files})\\n`)\n```\n\nV-007-4 expects exactly two lines for date-now fixture: `FAIL seams.date_now lib/bad.mjs:<line>` and `check-seams: FAILED (1 violations)`. Good.\n\nAlso \"以模块导入时无副作用\" → invokedDirectly guard.\n\nNow write it.\n\nOne more: design says count files even when violations exist in the summary? The FAILED line has no file count. OK.\n\nLet me write.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b7152cb2753b9f50a0ddd8a56816","_messageId":"01a1b7188bd874418147b7eed5c4518e","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b7152cb2753b9f50a0ddd8a56816","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b7188bd874418147b7eed5c4518e","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b7188bd874418147b7eed5c4518e","codebuddy.ai/messageId":"01a1b718-8bd8-7441-8147-b7f0defade56","traceparent":"00-ab582e66778689ad02f0c1bc3f32c494-923d627b0a0b3bd8-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b715-27ed-7fbb-b7b5-ecb1f10e3c39,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b7152cb2753b9f50a0ddd8a56816"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T17:40:26.230Z"}
