{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b71a-7695-7a5e-8852-e1e481c5e382","session_id":"01a1b715-27ed-7fbb-b7b5-ecb1f10e3c39","message":{"id":"01a1b71a76957a5e8852e1e293cb9385","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now integration/registration.test.mjs.\n\nTests:\n1. createPlugin registers four routes and reports ok\n2. partial failure reported, others still registered, stderr hint\n3. inject failure → null path\n4. apply strict throws; non-strict doesn't\n5. handler round-trip via injected ctx\n6. getRegistration before any apply → failed 'plugin not applied'\n\nBut getRegistration default test must run before any createPlugin in the same process. Node test files run in one process; test execution order is sequential by default within a file. But test #6 would run after #1-#5 which already mutated module state. So I can't test the \"never called\" default after. Put it first. But even then, module import order... it's fine as first test in the file. But if node runs tests concurrently? By default `node --test` runs test files in parallel processes but tests within a file run sequentially (unless concurrency).\n\nHmm, risk: the first test in the file might not be the first executed? They're executed in order. OK. But safer: don't test the untouched default (it's not in acceptance). Design §6 mentions it though. I'll include it as the first test.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: the module is imported fresh per process; each test file is a separate process. So placing the default-state test first works.\n\nHandler round-trip: createPlugin with fake ctx and configPath, then request providers/models.\n\nLet me write.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b7152cb2753b9f50a0